## 1. Choice of methods

### Topic modeling: BERTopic

BERTopic was chosen over LDA. LDA works on word counts only, so it treats "LLM" and "language model" as unrelated, and it is known to work poorly on short texts such as abstracts. BERTopic clusters the sentence embeddings the project already has, so it groups papers by meaning and no extra embedding pass is needed.

The pipeline is:

1. UMAP reduces the 384-dimensional embeddings to 5 dimensions (`n_neighbors=15`, `min_dist=0.0`, cosine distance, `random_state=42`).
2. HDBSCAN clusters the reduced vectors (`min_cluster_size=20`). Papers that do not belong to any cluster are put in topic -1.
3. c-TF-IDF picks the words that best describe each cluster. The top three words become the topic label.

The vectorizer removes English stop words plus a list of words that appear in almost every abstract ("propose", "method", "results", "framework" and similar), keeps one- and two-word phrases, and ignores words found in fewer than 5 papers.

`random_state` is fixed so the topics are the same on every run. Without it, the manual review would not match the next run.

### Entity extraction: NER instead of sentiment

The task allowed either sentiment analysis or NER. Sentiment was not used because research abstracts are written in a neutral tone, so a sentiment model would label almost every paper "neutral" and give no useful filter. Named entities (organizations, models, datasets, languages) are useful for filtering, for example "papers that mention Qwen3".

Five entity types are kept:

| Label | Meaning | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| `ORG` | companies, labs, universities | OpenAI, Google DeepMind, MIT |
| `PRODUCT` | named models, datasets, software, benchmarks | GPT-4, Qwen3, ImageNet, MMLU |
| `PERSON` | people | |
| `GPE` | countries and cities | U.S., UK |
| `LANGUAGE` | human languages | English, Arabic |

Numbers, dates, percentages and other types are dropped because they are not useful for filtering.

spaCy's small English model was used as the base because it is fast on CPU and already installed. Larger options were not used for this stage: `en_core_web_trf` is much slower on CPU, and zero-shot models such as GLiNER add another large model to the project.

